The regression was a simple inverted condition in `NameAliasMixin.get_real_name()`. The original buggy code was:

```python
def get_real_name(self):
    dot_idx = None
    for idx, tok in enumerate(self.tokens):
        if not (tok.match(T.Punctuation, '.')):
            dot_idx = idx
    return self._get_first_name(dot_idx, real_name=True)
```

This set `dot_idx` to the index of the last *non*-dot token (effectively almost always the very last token, e.g. an alias), instead of the index of the last dot punctuation token. As a result, for any dotted identifier that also had an alias (e.g. `foo.bar baz`, `foo.bar AS baz`), `get_real_name()` incorrectly returned the alias/trailing token's name instead of the actual real name, and this cascaded into failures across identifier/alias/grouping tests (get_name(), get_alias() consistency checks, etc., including cases asserting `get_alias()` is None where the mis-parsed state produced unexpected comparisons).

Fixed it to correctly find the index of the *last* dot token and look up the real name starting right after it:

```python
def get_real_name(self):
    """Returns the real name (object name) of this identifier."""
    # a.b.c -> real name is the component after the *last* dot
    dot_idx = None
    for idx, tok in enumerate(self.tokens):
        if tok.match(T.Punctuation, '.'):
            dot_idx = idx
    start_idx = dot_idx + 1 if dot_idx is not None else None
    return self._get_first_name(start_idx, real_name=True)
```

Verified manually against a range of cases (plain identifiers, `a.b`, `a.b.c`, `foo.bar baz`, `foo.bar AS baz`, `foo AS bar`, `foo bar`) and all now report correct `real_name`/`alias` pairs (e.g. `a.b.c` -> real_name `c`; `foo.bar baz` -> real_name `bar`, alias `baz`; plain `a` -> real_name `a`, alias `None`). No test directory exists in this checkout (hidden upstream tests will be used for grading), so I validated via ad hoc Python snippets through the `run` tool.

